# Plan probe purity

`Applicable` and `Check` on a `scaffold.Step` are pure predicates. They are called concurrently during the probe pass. A cold cache and a hot cache MUST produce the same verdict.

## Rules

- Do NOT call `PlanCacheSet`, `RecordPlanMachine*`, or any other cache-writing helper from inside `Applicable` or `Check`.
- Do NOT mutate `t.Payload` (e.g. `mt.Instance = ...`) from inside `Applicable` or `Check`.
- Do NOT swallow probe errors as `false, nil`. Return `false, err` so the preview can render "unknown" instead of lying as "will execute".
- World-touching work (provider `ListByTag`, SSH probes, headscale CLI, preauth-key derivation) goes in a `Resolve*()` helper, not in `Check`.

## The Resolve pattern

A `Resolve*()` helper MAY be cached, but MUST NOT depend on the cache being warm:

1. Check the plan cache (or a typed field on the payload).
2. On hit → return the cached value.
3. On miss → do the work, write the result back, return it.

Same verdict regardless of call order. Concurrency-safe via singleflight or `sync.Once`.

Reference implementation — copy this shape:

```go
// internal/claws/plans/apply/mesh/control_url.go
func getOrResolveControlURL(ctx context.Context, dial SSHDialFunc, mt *MeshTarget) (string, error) {
    if v, ok := scaffold.PlanCacheGet(ctx, CacheKeyControlURL); ok {
        if s, _ := v.(string); s != "" {
            return s, nil
        }
    }
    url, err := resolveControlURL(ctx, dial, mt, mt.Gateway)
    if err != nil {
        return "", fmt.Errorf("resolve control URL: %w", err)
    }
    scaffold.PlanCacheSet(ctx, CacheKeyControlURL, url)
    return url, nil
}
```

## Anti-patterns to avoid

- `create_machine.go:Check` writing `mt.Instance`, `RecordPlanMachineExists`, `RecordPlanMachineHost` — makes probe ordering-dependent.
- `install_tailscale.go:Check` writing `RecordPlanMachineMeshIP` — same problem.
- `wait_cloud_init.go:Check` returning `false, nil` on every error path — hides SSH failures as "will execute".
- Check methods that only read a cache their own Execute populates (e.g. `resolve_control_url.Check`, `resolve_preauth_key.Check`) — always misreport "will execute" on cold runs.

Prefer resolving the actual underlying state every time, cached for cheapness.
